 "pair": "ru-en",
 "title": "Ссора перед отъездом  (An Argument Before Leaving)",
 "level": "A1 — Duolingo Section 2",
 "labels": {
  "max": "Макс",
  "vika": "Вика"
 },
 "segments": [
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": "Vika has been about to fly to Milan for work, and Max has been avoiding her all day. He finally corners her before she leaves. Hear the argument in Russian first.",
   "gap": 0.8
  },
  {
   "v": "max",
   "t": "Почему ты не сказала, что уезжаешь?",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "vika",
   "t": "Давай позже поговорим.",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "max",
   "t": "Нет, поговорим сейчас!",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "max",
   "t": "Разреши мне объяснить, в чём дело.",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "vika",
   "t": "Ты весь день от меня бегаешь!",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "vika",
   "t": "Мне надо подумать.",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "vika",
   "t": "Поговорим через три дня, когда прилечу.",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "max",
   "t": "Вика, доброе утро!",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "max",
   "t": "Я тебе кофе принёс.",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "vika",
   "t": "Я тебя ненавижу!",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "max",
   "t": "Ты не любишь кофе?",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "vika",
   "t": "Да какой к чёрту кофе!",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "vika",
   "t": "Я из-за тебя не полетела в Милан!",
   "gap": 0.9
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": "Line by line, to follow Vika's mood.",
   "gap": 0.4
  },
  {
   "v": "max",
   "t": "Почему ты не сказала, что уезжаешь?",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": "'Why didn't you tell me you were leaving?' We can break down this line: ",
   "gap": 0.25
  },
  {
   "v": "max",
   "t": "сказала",
   "gap": 0.25
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": " takes a woman's ending given the person spoken to is Vika. ",
   "gap": 0.25
  },
  {
   "v": "max",
   "t": "уезжаешь",
   "gap": 0.25
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": ", you're leaving, has a prefix for 'away', on an action for going by vehicle.",
   "gap": 0.4
  },
  {
   "v": "vika",
   "t": "Давай позже поговорим.",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": "'Let's talk later.' We can consider the phrase: ",
   "gap": 0.25
  },
  {
   "v": "vika",
   "t": "Давай",
   "gap": 0.25
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": " begins a suggestion. ",
   "gap": 0.25
  },
  {
   "v": "vika",
   "t": "позже",
   "gap": 0.25
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": " is 'later', and ",
   "gap": 0.25
  },
  {
   "v": "vika",
   "t": "поговорим",
   "gap": 0.25
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": " is 'let's have a talk'. Vika wants the talk to happen, but not now.",
   "gap": 0.4
  },
  {
   "v": "max",
   "t": "Нет, поговорим сейчас!",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": "'No, we're talking now!' It's the same ",
   "gap": 0.25
  },
  {
   "v": "max",
   "t": "поговорим",
   "gap": 0.25
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": " as before, and the only thing that changes is ",
   "gap": 0.25
  },
  {
   "v": "max",
   "t": "сейчас",
   "gap": 0.25
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": ", 'now'. Max copies her words, which is a good way to answer.",
   "gap": 0.4
  },
  {
   "v": "max",
   "t": "Разреши мне объяснить, в чём дело.",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": "'Let me explain what's going on.' Here we have for 'what's going on', ",
   "gap": 0.25
  },
  {
   "v": "max",
   "t": "в",
   "gap": 0.25
  },
  {
   "v": "max",
   "t": "чём",
   "gap": 0.25
  },
  {
   "v": "max",
   "t": "дело",
   "gap": 0.25
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": ", reads 'in what is the matter'. It's a set expression, so learn it as a lump and use it whenever you want an explanation.",
   "gap": 0.4
  },
  {
   "v": "vika",
   "t": "Ты весь день от меня бегаешь!",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": "'You've been avoiding me all day!' 'Running around' is how Russian says 'avoiding', so she says you run ",
   "gap": 0.25
  },
  {
   "v": "vika",
   "t": "от",
   "gap": 0.25
  },
  {
   "v": "vika",
   "t": "меня",
   "gap": 0.25
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": ". English uses 'have been' for this, but Russian just uses the present.",
   "gap": 0.4
  },
  {
   "v": "vika",
   "t": "Мне надо подумать.",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": "'I need to think.' We can inspect the dialogue: ",
   "gap": 0.25
  },
  {
   "v": "vika",
   "t": "подумать",
   "gap": 0.25
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": " has a prefix that makes the thinking short, as in 'think for a bit'. ",
   "gap": 0.25
  },
  {
   "v": "vika",
   "t": "Мне",
   "gap": 0.25
  },
  {
   "v": "vika",
   "t": "надо",
   "gap": 0.25
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": " is 'to me it's necessary', Russian for 'I need'.",
   "gap": 0.4
  },
  {
   "v": "vika",
   "t": "Поговорим через три дня, когда прилечу.",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": "'We'll talk in three days when I get back.' The prefix on ",
   "gap": 0.25
  },
  {
   "v": "vika",
   "t": "прилечу",
   "gap": 0.25
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": " means 'arrive', and the rest is the action of flying. So Vika says 'I'll arrive by air', which suits a plane trip.",
   "gap": 0.4
  },
  {
   "v": "max",
   "t": "Вика, доброе утро!",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": "'Vika, good morning!' The term for morning is a neuter word, so 'good' becomes ",
   "gap": 0.25
  },
  {
   "v": "max",
   "t": "доброе",
   "gap": 0.25
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": ". Here, and on the coffee line, Max talks as if nothing has happened.",
   "gap": 0.4
  },
  {
   "v": "max",
   "t": "Я тебе кофе принёс.",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": "'I brought you coffee.' Notice order is 'to you coffee brought', and Russian is perfectly happy with that. ",
   "gap": 0.25
  },
  {
   "v": "max",
   "t": "принёс",
   "gap": 0.25
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": " is 'brought', and ",
   "gap": 0.25
  },
  {
   "v": "max",
   "t": "кофе",
   "gap": 0.25
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": " never changes its ending.",
   "gap": 0.4
  },
  {
   "v": "vika",
   "t": "Я тебя ненавижу!",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": "'I hate you!' Let us look at the sentence: ",
   "gap": 0.25
  },
  {
   "v": "vika",
   "t": "ненавижу",
   "gap": 0.25
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": " is 'I hate', and it's every bit as strong as the English. ",
   "gap": 0.25
  },
  {
   "v": "vika",
   "t": "тебя",
   "gap": 0.25
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": " is the form for 'you' on the receiving end.",
   "gap": 0.4
  },
  {
   "v": "max",
   "t": "Ты не любишь кофе?",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": "'You don't like coffee?' Max is baffled. ",
   "gap": 0.25
  },
  {
   "v": "max",
   "t": "любишь",
   "gap": 0.25
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": " is 'you like' or 'you love', depending on what's coming. For liking a thing, Russians also use a different phrase meaning 'to you it is pleasing'.",
   "gap": 0.4
  },
  {
   "v": "vika",
   "t": "Да какой к чёрту кофе!",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": "'What the hell coffee!' We can break down this part: ",
   "gap": 0.25
  },
  {
   "v": "vika",
   "t": "какой",
   "gap": 0.25
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": " plus a naming word gives a scornful 'what do you mean, ...!'. ",
   "gap": 0.25
  },
  {
   "v": "vika",
   "t": "к",
   "gap": 0.25
  },
  {
   "v": "vika",
   "t": "чёрту",
   "gap": 0.25
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": " means 'to the devil', a mild way of dismissing the coffee.",
   "gap": 0.4
  },
  {
   "v": "vika",
   "t": "Я из-за тебя не полетела в Милан!",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": "'I missed the Milan trip because of you!' We can consider the speech: ",
   "gap": 0.25
  },
  {
   "v": "vika",
   "t": "не",
   "gap": 0.25
  },
  {
   "v": "vika",
   "t": "полетела",
   "gap": 0.25
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": " ends in a special sound for a woman. Here we have 'because of you' is the one Russians use for blame. Now the argument, uncut.",
   "gap": 0.9
  },
  {
   "v": "max",
   "t": "Почему ты не сказала, что уезжаешь?",
   "gap": 0.5
  },
  {
   "v": "vika",
   "t": "Давай позже поговорим.",
   "gap": 0.5
  },
  {
   "v": "max",
   "t": "Нет, поговорим сейчас!",
   "gap": 0.5
